<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Sacred War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/356-bce">356</a>–346 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Sacred War of 356–346 BCE was fought between the Phocians, who seized the sanctuary of Delphi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delphic-amphictyony">Delphic Amphictyony</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, the Thessalians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a>. The Phocians paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> with the temple treasures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> ended the war in 346 BCE, gaining a seat on the Amphictyonic Council and influence in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-sacred-war.jpg" alt="Third Sacred War" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fingalo · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:07Delphi_Theater03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 357 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delphic-amphictyony">Amphictyonic League</a>, the religious body that governed the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Delphi and was then dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, fined the Phocian Confederation for cultivating sacred land on the Kirrhaean plain. The Phocians did not deny the offence, but the fine was far beyond their means, and refusing it would make them outcasts liable to a sacred war. The main ancient account is the Bibliotheca historica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, written in the 1st century BCE, which leaves many gaps and gives little firm chronology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In about July 356 BCE the Phocian leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philomelus">Philomelus</a> seized Delphi and its sanctuary, killed the nobles of the Thrakidai family and routed a relief force of Ozolian Locrians, whose captives he had thrown from the Phaidriadai rocks, the traditional punishment for sacrilege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> then persuaded most Greek states to declare a sacred war; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a>. To pay ever larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> armies the Phocians plundered the dedications of the temple, spending an estimated 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>'s treasure.</p><p>That wealth let them absorb heavy defeats, among them the battles of Neon and the Crocus Field, and fight on under Onomarchos and then his brother Phayllos. The war spread into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dori">Doris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. Called into Thessaly by the ruling clan of Larissa against Pherae, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> won at the Crocus Field in 352 BCE and became ruler of Thessaly. By 347 BCE the Phocians were running out of treasure and the Thebans could not defeat them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 346 BCE Athens made the Peace of Philocrates with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, while Philip's troops quietly seized the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>. By 12 July the Athenians knew he was in the gates and reaffirmed the peace, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> without help. The Phocian cities were destroyed and a heavy fine imposed on the confederation, which was readmitted to the Amphictyony only in 279 BCE. The settlement marked a major step in Macedon's rise to pre-eminence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and many offerings of the sanctuary had been lost for good.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Sacred War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The gravest blow came in the <b>Third Sacred War</b> of 356–346 BCE, when the Phocians under Philomelus seized Delphi and his successor Onomarchus spent the temple treasures on mercenaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Third Sacred War</b> against Phocis (356-346 BCE) brought Philip II of Macedon onto the council, and the Fourth (339 BCE) led to his victory at Chaeronea.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li><li><blockquote>The Phocian League overran much of the region in the <b>Third Sacred War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/opuntian-locris">Opuntian Locris</a></li></ul></section>
